  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday night through next Monday)\nIssued at 231 PM CDT Mon May 11 2026\n\nThe main theme of the long term forecast will be a return to \nwarmer than normal temperatures. By Wednesday, upper level ridging\nwill set up over Texas. This ridging will slowly move east during\nthe second half of the week, and into the eastern U. S. by this \nweekend. In addition to the upper level ridging, a low level \nthermal ridge will spread east into west-central Texas, warming \n850mb temperatures from 10 to 15 degrees Celsius today into the 20\nto 25 degree range by Wednesday. The low level thermal ridge axis\nis expected to be oriented from the Davis Mountains area \nnortheast across the Permian Basin, into southwestern Oklahoma. So\nwhile most of our area won't feel the full brunt of this thermal \nridge, we will see temperatures warm into the upper 80s to mid 90s\nWednesday through next weekend, with the hottest temperatures \nexpected across the Big Country and northwestern Concho Valley. \nHighs could near the triple digits for areas west of a \nThrockmorton to Abilene to San Angelo line Thursday through next \nweekend. \n\nThere is less certainty with the forecast from Sunday into early \nnext week as models show some disagreement. However, there will be\nintermittent low (20%) chances for showers and thunderstorms \nSaturday afternoon into early next week.",
